+/// Anchor the session clock, if it is not already anchored.
+///
+/// # Why this exists
+///
+/// `main` calls this once, before dispatch, so the session's anchor is the
+/// wall reading closest to when the *process* started rather than whichever
+/// reading happened to be first asked for. The two used to be the same thing
+/// in practice — most commands ask for the time, if at all, within a moment
+/// of starting — but `-` on stdin (F2) reads every line before dispatch runs
+/// even once, and a caller typing at a pipe for a minute would have anchored
+/// the session a minute late.
+///
+/// # Idempotent
+///
+/// Only the first call anchors anything; [`session_now`] and every later call
+/// here reuse that anchor exactly as before. A build without a command that
+/// ever asks the time now pays for one clock read it did not used to, which
+/// is the trade this makes for the session's anchor meaning "when the program
+/// started" rather than "whenever it was first asked".
+#[cfg(all(feature = "std", feature = "civil"))]
+pub fn anchor_at_start() -> Result<(), TimeError> {
+    session_now().map(|_| ())
+}
